{"name":"Country Blue","hex":"#717f9b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"country","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The territory of a nation; a sovereign state or a region once independent and still distinct in institutions, language, etc. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A large area regarded as distinct from other areas, e.g. because it is inhabited by a specific group of people"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["From or in the countryside, connected with it, or typical of it.","Of or connected to country music."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/country"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Country Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Country Blue is a 1973 American independent romantic crime drama film cowritten and directed by Jack Conrad and starring Conrad, Dub Taylor, Rita George, and David Huddleston. The film was inspired by actual bank robberies which took place at a Farmers Merchant Bank."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/46/Country_Blue_1973_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country_Blue","wikidata":"Q141191703"},{"title":"Country","blocks":[{"text":"A country is a political entity or its territory in the world. They function as sovereign states, unrecognized states, constituent countries, or dependent territories. A country may consist of multiple nations, a structure known as a multinational state. Countries are often distinguished as developing countries or developed countries. There is no universal agreement on the number of \"countries\" in the world. This ambiguity is a result of several countries not being recognized as sovereign states by the UN system, but are recognized by at least one UN member, known as \"diplomatic recognition\". These countries often have disputed territories and have only partial recognition. A number of entities without recognition are also commonly considered a country."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Official_United_Nations_World_Map_-_October_2022.svg/330px-Official_United_Nations_World_Map_-_October_2022.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country","wikidata":"Q6256"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg/500px-Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg/500px-Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A number of non-sovereign entities nevertheless have country codes, such as PF (French Polynesia) and TK (Tokelau)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg/500px-Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg/1280px-Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg/500px-Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early version of the \"Wilhelmus\" as preserved in a manuscript of 1617 (Brussels, Royal Library, MS 15662, fol. 37v-38r)[77]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHandschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Countries_by_GDP_%28PPP%29_per_capita_in_2020.png/500px-Countries_by_GDP_%28PPP%29_per_capita_in_2020.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Countries_by_GDP_%28PPP%29_per_capita_in_2020.png/1280px-Countries_by_GDP_%28PPP%29_per_capita_in_2020.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Countries_by_GDP_%28PPP%29_per_capita_in_2020.png/500px-Countries_by_GDP_%28PPP%29_per_capita_in_2020.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gross domestic product per capita of 213 \"countries\" (2020) (pur